.pdxdoc  libpdx-elevate  1.0.0
.audience  tool-author, security-auditor, kernel-integrator
.format-version  1
.rendered-by  doc (§5.3 of design/tooling/r49-r50-plan.md)

.section  NAME

libpdx-elevate — client-side elevate-protocol helper for per-op
capability requests on PaideiaOS.

.section  SYNOPSIS

  ;; assembly-level (from src/*.pdx)
  call  elevate_client_lookup_broker
  call  elevate_client_request_ex_r   ;; full-featured: retry + audit + policy
  call  elevate_client_cap_check_and_revoke

  ;; conceptual (tool-side)
  hold  Cap<KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(read,  "svc.elevate-broker")>
  hold  Cap<KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(write, broker_ep)>
  hold  Cap<KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT(mint,  reply)>
  request  caps=<mask>, dur=<ns>, target_fp_lo=<user>
  receive  Cap<KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL(0x191, expire_ns)>

.section  DESCRIPTION

libpdx-elevate wraps the R48.M7 kernel elevate protocol
(`src/kernel/core/ipc/elevate_channel.pdx`) with the client-side state
machine tools need to request a bounded-lifetime capability from
`svc.elevate-broker`.  It is a shared library — no binary, no daemon.
Consumers are `pkg` (§5.1, elevate for `/pkgs` write), the R50
coreutils (`cp` and `mv` for cross-subtree destinations), and every
future tool that needs to escalate a single operation without becoming
setuid or claiming persistent supervisor authority.

The library does not replace `sudo`.  There is no `sudo` in PaideiaOS
(see .see-also §5 of design/user/model.md).  What replaces it is the
per-op elevate protocol: for one operation, at one moment, with one
duration budget and one cap-mask scope, the caller asks the founder (or
the auto-approve policy table) for a `Cap<KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL>`.  The
cap dies at its expire_ns.  No lingering root shell exists to hijack;
no persistent group membership grants standing authority; every request
is journaled to `/system/audit/user-events/`.

.section  INVARIANTS  I4  I5  I7  D3  D4

Every entry point in libpdx-elevate enforces the following:

  D3 (audit-first)
    Every mutating flow (request_ex_j, request_ex_r) writes its REQ
    audit record before it reaches the broker.  A REQ-journal failure
    aborts the operation with ELVJ_ERR_REQ_JOURNAL_FAIL — no wire hop
    occurs.  Successful APR writes its APR audit record before the
    cap is returned to the caller.

  D4 (dual-signed manifest)
    libpdx-elevate ships with manifest.pdxsig carrying author +
    Paideia-root ML-DSA-65 signatures.  `pkg install libpdx-elevate`
    verifies both before the .pdx sources are placed under /pkgs/.

  I4 (never silent failure)
    Every entry point returns a nonzero status code on the failure
    path (see .section STATUS CODES).  No path silently returns
    success on partial completion.

  I5 (destructive ops undoable)
    Not applicable at library level — libpdx-elevate holds no state
    on the caller's behalf.  The Cap<KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL> lifetime
    bound (M2-003) is the equivalent invariant: the cap self-
    invalidates at expire_ns and calls to it thereafter fail with
    ELCC_STATE_REVOKED.  A caller cannot "forget" to release it.

  I7 (help + tutorial + example-gallery)
    This .pdxdoc file is the doc(1) back-end.  Callers hit --help
    against a consuming tool (e.g. `pkg --help`), which references
    this doc via .see-also.  --tutorial and --examples for
    libpdx-elevate specifically will land with the doc tool itself
    (§5.3, doc.M2); today the .examples section below stands in.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_lookup_broker

.signature  fn elevate_client_lookup_broker() -> ep_id | ELVC_ERR_LOOKUP_FAIL

Resolves the canonical name `svc.elevate-broker` to a
KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT handle via `svc_lookup`.  No side effects; safe to
call at any point in the caller's lifecycle.  The returned handle is
suitable as the `broker_ep_id` argument to `elevate_client_send_req`.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_set_human_timeout / _set_fast_timeout

.signature  fn elevate_client_set_human_timeout(ns: u64)
.signature  fn elevate_client_set_fast_timeout(ns: u64)

Set the recv-timeout budget for the human-approve and auto-approve
paths respectively.  Defaults are 30 s and 500 ms.  Callers that
raise the human budget above 30 s should also raise their own
user-visible progress indication — the founder may take that long to
respond, and the terminal will otherwise appear stuck.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_request_ex

.signature  fn elevate_client_request_ex(caps: u64, dur: u64, req_buf: *u8,
.signature                               reply_ep_id: u32, reply_buf: *u8,
.signature                               timeout_ns: u64) -> ELVC_*

The full M2 flow.  Sequence: policy consult (fast/human classification)
→ REQ assemble via `elevate_request_write_frame` → REQ send via
`elevate_client_send_req` → APR recv via `elevate_client_recv_reply` →
grant subset check via `elevate_client_check_grant`.
`timeout_ns == 0` picks the human or fast default based on the policy
classification.  `target_fp_lo` comes from the process-global set via
`elevate_client_set_target_fp_lo`.  Caller must supply a
64-byte-aligned `req_buf` and `reply_buf`.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_request_ex_j

.signature  fn elevate_client_request_ex_j(caps: u64, dur: u64, ...)
.signature                                 -> ELVC_*/ELVJ_ERR_*

M3-001 audit-first wrapper.  Journals REQ before wire hop; aborts on
REQ-journal failure; journals APR on success; returns
ELVJ_ERR_APR_JOURNAL_FAIL if the APR-journal write fails after grant
(caller should revoke immediately in that case — the audit chain is
broken otherwise).

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_request_ex_r

.signature  fn elevate_client_request_ex_r(caps: u64, dur: u64, ...)
.signature                                 -> ELVC_*/ELVJ_ERR_*/ELVR_ERR_*

M3-002 retry wrapper over _ex_j.  Retriable set is
{ELVC_ERR_TIMEOUT, ELVC_ERR_SEND_FAIL, ELVC_ERR_LOOKUP_FAIL}.  Bounds
[1, 8] attempts (default 3).  Backoff 100 ms → 400 ms → 1.6 s
(capped).  Every attempt writes its own REQ audit record; the eventual
success writes its APR record; the auditor sees a per-attempt trail.
Non-retriable failures pass through unchanged on the first attempt.

.section  ENTRY  elevate_client_cap_mint / _bind_expire / _check_and_revoke

.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_mint(...) -> Cap<KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL>
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_bind_expire(row_id: u32, dur_ns: u64)
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_expired(row_id: u32) -> u32
.signature  fn elevate_client_cap_check_and_revoke(row_id: u32) -> ELCC_*

M2-003 cap-lifetime wrapper.  Kernel default `expire_ns == 0` is
compensated by a client-side shadow deadline map (16 slots, one per
possible row_id).  `check_and_revoke` invokes kernel
`elevate_channel_cap_revoke` when past deadline; the call is
idempotent (`REVOKE_ALREADY` collapses to `STATE_REVOKED`).

Callers should invoke `check_and_revoke` at every re-entry to a
guarded region; the M4 witness `elevate_client_cap_witness`
demonstrates the pattern (12 stages, fingerprint
`LIBPDX-ELEVATE M4 CAP OK`).

.section  STATUS  CODES

Codes are grouped by band; the numeric value is a u32 return code.
Every entry point returns 0 on success.

  0xFFFFEA01  ELVC_ERR_TIMEOUT           recv-timeout expired (retriable)
  0xFFFFEA02  ELVC_ERR_BAD_REPLY         reply frame malformed (non-retriable)
  0xFFFFEA03  ELVC_ERR_GRANT_INVALID     granted caps not subset of requested
  0xFFFFEA04  ELVC_ERR_BAD_EXPIRE        expire_ns == 0 in APR
  0xFFFFEA05  ELVC_ERR_BAD_REPLY_EP      caller-supplied reply_ep_id invalid
  0xFFFFEA10..1F  ELCP_ERR_*             policy table (BAD_ARG, RATE_LIMIT,
                                         NO_MATCH, ENOSPC, BAD_DUR, BAD_CAPS)
  0xFFFFEA20  ELVR_ERR_EXHAUSTED         retry attempts exhausted
  0xFFFFEA21  ELVR_ERR_BAD_ARG           retry config out of bounds
  0xFFFFEA30..3F  ELCC_*                 cap-lifetime (BAD_ROW, MINT_FAIL,
                                         NO_DEADLINE, REVOKE_FAIL, STATE_LIVE,
                                         EXPIRED, REVOKED, NOTE_NO_NARROW)
  0xFFFFEA40  ELVJ_ERR_BAD_ACTOR         no target_fp_lo set (process-global)
  0xFFFFEA41  ELVJ_ERR_REQ_JOURNAL_FAIL  audit REQ write failed; abort
  0xFFFFEA42  ELVJ_ERR_APR_JOURNAL_FAIL  audit APR write failed; revoke grant

Consumers map these to the shared exit codes (design/tooling/plan.md
§4.1 I4 table): every ELVC_/ELCP_/ELCC_ failure is exit 4
(capability denied); ELVR_ERR_EXHAUSTED is exit 3 (system error);
ELVJ_ERR_* is exit 4 (capability + audit denied).

.section  EXAMPLES

  ;; #1 — happy-path per-op elevate, auto-approve
  ;;
  ;; A tool wants to write /pkgs for a package install.  target_fp_lo
  ;; is the founder's user fingerprint.  Caller pre-mints a reply
  ;; endpoint and a request buffer.
  ;;
  ;;   caps       = 0x0000000000000002  ;; KIND_PDXFS_FILE(write, /pkgs)
  ;;   dur        = 60_000_000_000       ;; 60 s
  ;;   timeout_ns = 0                    ;; use policy default
  ;;
  ;;   elevate_client_set_target_fp_lo   founder_fp_lo
  ;;   elevate_client_request_ex_r       caps, dur, req_buf, reply_ep,
  ;;                                     reply_buf, timeout_ns
  ;;   ;; on success rax=0, reply_buf holds APR frame

  ;; #2 — cap-lifetime enforcement at re-entry
  ;;
  ;;   loop:
  ;;     elevate_client_cap_check_and_revoke   row_id
  ;;     cmp     rax, ELCC_STATE_LIVE
  ;;     jne     .cap_expired_or_bad
  ;;     ;; ...perform guarded work using the cap...
  ;;     jmp     loop
  ;;   .cap_expired_or_bad:
  ;;     ;; cap dead — either re-request or bail

.section  POSIX  DIFFERENCES

The Unix analog is `sudo`.  libpdx-elevate is different on five
observable axes:

  1. sudo grants a shell.  libpdx-elevate grants a cap for one op.
  2. sudo persists for a tty timeout (default 5 min).  libpdx-elevate
     caps carry an explicit expire_ns; the kernel invalidates at
     deadline; no session-time inheritance.
  3. sudo authenticates once and remembers.  libpdx-elevate consults
     the policy table for auto-approve, or blocks on the founder for
     human approve, per invocation.  There is no "sudo timestamp".
  4. sudo works via setuid + /etc/sudoers.  libpdx-elevate mints a
     kernel-checked `Cap<KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL>` — the kernel enforces
     the scope; no in-kernel superuser exists to bypass.  See §3.3
     of design/user/model.md ("Founder is not magic").
  5. sudo logs to /var/log/auth.log optionally.  libpdx-elevate
     journals every REQ and every APR to
     /system/audit/user-events/ before the wire hop (D3 audit-first).

